trace.content:
[THOUGHT] The patient presents with a 3-week history of fatigue, low-grade fever, abdominal pain, nausea, and dark urine — a constellation highly suggestive of hepatobiliary pathology, particularly hepatitis (viral, autoimmune, or toxic) or intrahepatic/extrahepatic cholestasis. Dark urine (due to conjugated bilirubinuria) plus fatigue, nausea, and abdominal discomfort raises concern for liver dysfunction. Key next steps:  
- Confirm presence of jaundice (clinical sign of hyperbilirubinemia) via physical exam;  
- Assess for hepatomegaly, tenderness, or stigmata of chronic liver disease;  
- Obtain objective liver function data — a Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) will provide ALT, AST, ALP, GGT, total and direct bilirubin, albumin, and INR — critical for characterizing hepatocellular vs. cholestatic injury and assessing synthetic function.  

Urinalysis is also valuable to confirm bilirubinuria (supports conjugated hyperbilirubinemia), but CMP is higher-yield as it integrates hepatic, renal, and metabolic assessment in one test.

[TEST] Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)

